><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250606104500+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250606104502+10'00'" name="d71f5115-772b-4c0c-8539-877e144f588b" page="13" coords="59.754300,742.527000,295.770000,742.527000,59.754300,730.851000,295.770000,730.851000,59.754300,729.375000,295.766000,729.375000,59.754300,717.699000,295.766000,717.699000,59.754300,716.165000,290.495000,716.165000,59.754300,704.489000,290.495000,704.489000,71.772700,703.183000,295.734000,703.183000,71.772700,691.507000,295.734000,691.507000,59.754300,690.031000,220.880000,690.031000,59.754300,678.355000,220.880000,678.355000,220.880000,690.031000,265.157000,690.029000,220.880000,677.496000,265.157000,677.496000,270.085000,690.029000,295.747000,690.029000,270.085000,678.353000,295.747000,678.353000,59.754500,676.820000,273.093000,676.820000,59.754500,665.144000,273.093000,665.144000,273.093000,676.820000,295.763000,676.820000,273.093000,664.286000,295.763000,664.286000,59.754500,663.668000,82.052200,663.668000,59.754500,651.133000,82.052200,651.133000,86.570400,663.667000,295.745000,663.667000,86.570400,651.991000,295.745000,651.991000,59.754200,650.515000,295.763000,650.515000,59.754200,638.839000,295.763000,638.839000,59.754200,637.306000,295.755000,637.306000,59.754200,625.630000,295.755000,625.630000,59.754200,624.153000,295.782000,624.153000,59.754200,612.477000,295.782000,612.477000,59.754200,611.001000,295.780000,611.001000,59.754200,599.325000,295.780000,599.325000,59.754200,597.792000,295.772000,597.792000,59.754200,586.116000,295.772000,586.116000,59.754200,584.639000,295.767000,584.639000,59.754200,572.963000,295.767000,572.963000,59.754200,571.487000,295.733000,571.487000,59.754200,559.811000,295.733000,559.811000,59.754200,558.278000,295.743000,558.278000,59.754200,546.602000,295.743000,546.602000,59.754200,545.125000,295.762000,545.125000,59.754200,533.449000,295.762000,533.449000,59.754200,531.973000,295.771000,531.973000,59.754200,520.297000,295.771000,520.297000,59.754200,518.764000,295.743000,518.764000,59.754200,507.088000,295.743000,507.088000,59.754200,505.611000,295.750000,505.611000,59.754200,493.935000,295.750000,493.935000,59.754200,492.402000,295.758000,492.402000,59.754200,480.726000,295.758000,480.726000,59.754200,479.250000,295.766000,479.250000,59.754200,467.574000,295.766000,467.574000,59.754200,466.097000,116.224000,466.097000,59.754200,454.421000,116.224000,454.421000" rect="59.362800,454.057000,296.155000,742.892000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: In this study, motion capture and SPH modelling&#xD;provided a means for studying the performance of an&#xD;asymmetrical freestyle swimming stroke. It was shown&#xD;that one-sided breathing of the freestyle swimmer&#xD;causes measurable differences in factors which are&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>282 R. C. Z. COHEN ET AL.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>known to have a significant impact on the performance,&#xD;namely the arm kinematics, hydrodynamic flow&#xD;structures, hand trajectory and hand angles of attack.&#xD;The swimmer breathing coincided with the right&#xD;arm underwater propulsive period (R</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>ctch</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>-R</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>out</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>) which&#xD;was shorter in duration than for the left arm (L</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>ctch</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>-&#xD;L</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>out</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). Additionally, the wrist trajectories in the reference&#xD;frame of the swimmer were visibly asymmetrical,&#xD;especially when viewed from front-on and side-on&#xD;perspectives. In comparison, the right arm had both a&#xD;larger mean propulsive force and a higher peak speed&#xD;achieved during the push. The vortex flow structures&#xD;associated with the right arm were stronger than for&#xD;the left arm. Finally, the hand angles of attack and&#xD;resultant forces were seen to display asymmetry, most&#xD;notably with the left hand having a ‘kink’ in its trajectory&#xD;which coincided with a drop off of propulsion.&#xD;The asymmetrical performance and the linkages back&#xD;through to the swimmer technique in this paper are&#xD;the key findings that will help to improve the decision&#xD;making of coaches and athletes seeking to improve&#xD;performance.</span
></p
></body
